1
00:00:01,950 --> 00:00:04,941
Every year, lakhs of students join AICTE-approved

2
00:00:04,941 --> 00:00:06,741
institutions.

3
00:00:06,741 --> 00:00:08,541
Behind each admission is a record that

4
00:00:08,541 --> 00:00:10,341
should be accurate,

5
00:00:10,341 --> 00:00:12,141
verifiable, and fair to every student.

6
00:00:12,141 --> 00:00:13,941
This is the APAAR Student Register.

7
00:00:20,950 --> 00:00:22,877
Today, oversight mostly sees totals.

8
00:00:22,877 --> 00:00:25,178
An institution reports how many students it

9
00:00:25,178 --> 00:00:26,978
admitted, not who they are.

10
00:00:26,978 --> 00:00:28,978
Excess admissions hide inside those numbers.

11
00:00:28,978 --> 00:00:30,798
The same student can appear at two

12
00:00:30,798 --> 00:00:32,598
colleges at once.

13
00:00:32,598 --> 00:00:34,398
And when an employer asks if a

14
00:00:34,398 --> 00:00:36,198
degree is real,

15
00:00:36,198 --> 00:00:37,998
the answer is a letter and a

16
00:00:37,998 --> 00:00:39,798
long wait.

17
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:53,248
So we built one register of student-level

18
00:00:53,248 --> 00:00:55,153
enrolments, keyed to the APAAR ID.

19
00:00:55,153 --> 00:00:57,227
Every record is checked the same way,

20
00:00:57,227 --> 00:00:59,188
and every seat is counted from real

21
00:00:59,188 --> 00:01:01,150
records, not from a declared total.

22
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:13,065
AICTE opens the national register.

23
00:01:13,065 --> 00:01:15,864
Every figure here is computed from individual

24
00:01:15,864 --> 00:01:17,664
records.

25
00:01:17,664 --> 00:01:19,968
Narmada's MBA programme shows sixty-eight students against

26
00:01:19,968 --> 00:01:21,768
an approved intake of sixty.

27
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:29,011
Institutions upload their admissions as a spreadsheet.

28
00:01:29,011 --> 00:01:31,506
Twenty-six validation rules check every row.

29
00:01:31,506 --> 00:01:33,717
A mistyped APAAR ID gets a did-you-mean

30
00:01:33,717 --> 00:01:35,701
suggestion from the registry check,

31
00:01:35,701 --> 00:01:37,798
which is simulated in this prototype.

32
00:01:37,798 --> 00:01:39,598
Staff fix the flagged rows,

33
00:01:39,598 --> 00:01:41,398
and a row with an error is

34
00:01:41,398 --> 00:01:43,198
never committed.

35
00:01:48,950 --> 00:01:50,750
Before admitting a student,

36
00:01:50,750 --> 00:01:52,550
the admission desk can run a seat

37
00:01:52,550 --> 00:01:54,350
check, and see whether one more admission

38
00:01:54,350 --> 00:01:56,150
would cross the approved intake.

39
00:02:00,950 --> 00:02:02,750
When a student is already active at

40
00:02:02,750 --> 00:02:05,105
another institution, the register raises a flag

41
00:02:05,105 --> 00:02:06,905
for review.

42
00:02:06,905 --> 00:02:08,705
The other institution's identity stays withheld,

43
00:02:08,705 --> 00:02:10,629
and name matching that understands Indian spelling

44
00:02:10,629 --> 00:02:13,721
variations finds possible duplicates without merging anything

45
00:02:13,721 --> 00:02:15,521
automatically.

46
00:02:17,950 --> 00:02:20,399
Institutions issue signed enrolment records.

47
00:02:20,399 --> 00:02:22,737
A student chooses exactly which details to

48
00:02:22,737 --> 00:02:24,537
share.

49
00:02:24,537 --> 00:02:26,337
An employer pastes the token and six

50
00:02:26,337 --> 00:02:28,137
checks run in order,

51
00:02:28,137 --> 00:02:29,937
from the signature to revocation.

52
00:02:29,937 --> 00:02:31,737
Change a single value,

53
00:02:31,737 --> 00:02:33,537
and verification fails.

54
00:02:33,537 --> 00:02:35,337
Consent-based requests work the same way,

55
00:02:35,337 --> 00:02:37,137
and only with the student's approval.

56
00:02:43,950 --> 00:02:46,012
Students under eighteen are protected.

57
00:02:46,012 --> 00:02:47,965
Their records stay on hold until the

58
00:02:47,965 --> 00:02:50,190
institution records a guardian's consent.

59
00:02:54,950 --> 00:02:57,315
Every action lands in a hash-chained audit

60
00:02:57,315 --> 00:02:59,173
trail that an auditor can verify.

61
00:02:59,173 --> 00:03:01,425
And a public status page states honestly

62
00:03:01,425 --> 00:03:03,225
what is built,

63
00:03:03,225 --> 00:03:05,025
what is simulated,

64
00:03:05,025 --> 00:03:06,825
and what still needs official access.

65
00:03:10,950 --> 00:03:12,750
Under the hood,

66
00:03:12,750 --> 00:03:14,550
a React interface talks to a FastAPI

67
00:03:14,550 --> 00:03:16,350
service.

68
00:03:16,350 --> 00:03:18,150
One validation engine is the only writer

69
00:03:18,150 --> 00:03:19,950
of student records.

70
00:03:19,950 --> 00:03:21,750
It feeds the seat ledger,

71
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:23,550
identity matching and signed credentials,

72
00:03:23,550 --> 00:03:25,350
with every step recorded in the audit

73
00:03:25,350 --> 00:03:27,150
chain, on PostgreSQL.

74
00:03:27,150 --> 00:03:28,950
The APAAR registry connection is simulated until

75
00:03:28,950 --> 00:03:30,750
official access is granted.

76
00:03:40,950 --> 00:03:42,750
APAAR Student Register.

77
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:44,550
Every seat counted,

78
00:03:44,550 --> 00:03:46,350
every student verifiable.

79
00:03:46,350 --> 00:03:48,150
Built for Smart India Hackathon,

80
00:03:48,150 --> 00:03:49,950
problem statement S-I-H two six two six

81
00:03:49,950 --> 00:03:51,750
three.
